
# Hashtable
## Definition
`Hashtable` is a **legacy**, **thread-safe**, hash table based implementation of the `Map` interface.
It stores data as **Key-Value pairs**, just like `HashMap`, but **all public methods are synchronized**, making it safe for multiple threads.
Unlike `HashMap`, `Hashtable` **does not allow null keys or null values**.
---

# Hierarchy

```text
                 Map
                  │
            Dictionary (Abstract Class)
                  │
              Hashtable
```

Unlike `HashMap`

```text
HashMap

Map
 │
AbstractMap
 │
HashMap
```

`Hashtable` extends **Dictionary**, while `HashMap` extends **AbstractMap**.

`Dictionary` is a legacy abstract class introduced before the Collections Framework (JDK 1.2).

---

# Why was Hashtable Introduced?

Before Java Collections Framework,

developers needed

* Fast searching
* Key-Value storage
* Thread safety

Java introduced

```text
Dictionary

↓

Hashtable
```

Later, Java introduced `HashMap`, which is faster but **not synchronized**.

Today,

```text
Single Thread

↓

HashMap
```

```text
Multi Thread

↓

ConcurrentHashMap (Recommended)
```

`Hashtable` is mostly maintained for **backward compatibility**.

---

# Internal Structure

Internally,

`Hashtable` is almost identical to `HashMap`.

It uses

* Hash Table (Bucket Array)
* Nodes (Entries)
* Hashing
* Collision handling

Difference

```text
HashMap

↓

Not synchronized
```

```text
Hashtable

↓

Every public method is synchronized
```

---

# Internal Working

The working is the **same as HashMap**.

Example

```java
table.put(101,"Java");
```

Steps

```text
Key

↓

hashCode()

↓

Bucket Index

↓

Store Entry
```

Searching

```java
table.get(101);
```

```text
Key

↓

hashCode()

↓

Bucket

↓

Return Value
```

---

# Internal Algorithm

The algorithms for

* `put()`
* `get()`
* `remove()`

are **the same as `HashMap`**.

The only additional step is

```text
Acquire Lock

↓

Execute Operation

↓

Release Lock
```

because every public method is synchronized.

---

# Time Complexity

| Operation     | Complexity |
| ------------- | ---------- |
| put()         | O(1)       |
| get()         | O(1)       |
| remove()      | O(1)       |
| containsKey() | O(1)       |

Worst Case

```text
O(n)
```

